	"identifying_description": "Leaf of paper with opaque watercolor and watercolor showing two seated women and one seated man at center. Outdoor scene on reverse. Carved and incised guards of bone and carved and painted sticks of bone. Bone or ivory pivot and copper alloy rivet.",
	"web_text": "For more than a century, inexpensive ornamental paper, called domino, was used by peasants to decorate their homes. Printed by woodblock and stenciled with color, the papers first served to decorate fireplace mantels, and later, to line drawers and trunks, to cover the end pages of books, to cover walls, and to adorn fan leaves. Many fan leaves produced by other methods imitated the domino style, as seen in this example. This fan's painted patterns evoke the complex weaves found in contemporary French silk textiles.",
	"didactic_label": "For more than a century, inexpensive ornamental paper, called domino, was used by peasants to decorate their homes. Printed by woodblock and stenciled with color, the papers first served to decorate fireplace mantels, and later, to line drawers and trunks, to cover the end pages of books, to cover walls, and to adorn fan leaves. Many fan leaves produced by other methods imitated the domino style, as seen in this example. This fan's painted patterns evoke the complex weaves found in contemporary French silk textiles.",
	"exhibition_history_text": "Fans of the Eighteenth Century, Textiles Education Gallery, de Young Museum, 9/25/18 - 7/719\nFans in Fashion, Legion of Honor, 5/23/81 - 8/30/81",
